ewcommand{\im}{\mathrm{i}\mkern1mu} 
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\cl}{\mathcal} 
ewcommand{\ud}{\mathrm{d}} 
ewcommand{\Rbb}{\mathbb{R}} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{	inv}[1]{ rac{1}{#1}}\] <blockquote> <p><strong>Definition. (Moreau envelope)</strong> Let $f: \Rbb^n ightarrow (-\infty, +\infty]$ be a proper, lower semi-continuous, convex function. For a parameter $\lambda &gt; 0$, the <strong>Moreau envelope</strong> of $f$ with index $\lambda$ is defined as</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:moreau} \mathrm{M}_\lambda f(x) = \inf_{y\in \Rbb^n} \left\{ f(y) + 	inv{2\lambda} \| x-y \|_2^2 ight\}. \end{equation}\] <p>This can be seen as a “<em>smoothed</em>” version of $f$, obtained by infimal convolution with the squared norm. Closely related is the <strong>proximal operator</strong>:</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:prox} \mathrm{prox}_{\lambda f} (x) := \mathrm{argmin}_{y \in \Rbb^n}  ig\{ f(y) + 	inv{2\lambda} \| x-y \|_2^2  ig\}. \end{equation}\] </blockquote> <p>From \eqref{eq:moreau} and \eqref{eq:prox}, the Moreau enveloppe can be expressed as:</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:3} \mathrm{M}_\lambda f(x) = f(\mathrm{prox}_{\lambda f} (x)) + 	inv{2\lambda} \| x-\mathrm{prox}_{\lambda f} (x) \|_2^2. \end{equation}\] <h2 id="example-the-absolute-value">Example: the absolute value:</h2> <table> <tbody> <tr> <td>Particularizing \eqref{eq:moreau} to $$f(x)=</td> <td>x</td> <td>$$ yields</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> \[ egin{align} \mathrm{prox}_{\lambda|\cdot|} (x) &amp;= \mathrm{argmin}_{y\in \Rbb^n} \left\{ |y| + 	inv{2 \lambda} (x-y)^2 ight\} \ &amp;= \mathrm{argmin}_{y\in \Rbb^n}  egin{cases} &amp;y + 	inv{2 \lambda} (x-y)^2,~~y&gt;0 \ &amp;-y + 	inv{2 \lambda} (x-y)^2,~~y&lt;0 \ \end{cases} \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow  egin{cases} &amp;\lambda + y-x = 0,~~y&gt;0 \ &amp;-\lambda + y-x = 0,~~y&lt;0 \ \end{cases} \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow  egin{cases} &amp;y = x-\lambda,~~x&gt;\lambda \ &amp;y = x+\lambda,~~x&lt;-\lambda \ &amp;y=0,~~\mathrm{otherwise.} \end{cases} \label{eq:7}. \end{align}\] <p>The solution in \eqref{eq:7} can be synthesized as:</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:soft} \mathrm{prox}_{\lambda|\cdot|} (x) = \mathrm{sign}(x) \max \{ |x| - \lambda, 0 \}. \end{equation}\] <p>The result in \eqref{eq:soft} is the 1-D case of the well-known <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proximal_gradient_methods_for_learning#Solving_for_L1_proximity_operator"><em>Soft-thresholding</em> operator</a> \(\cl S_\lambda = \mathrm{prox}_{\lambda \| \cdot \|_1}\).</p> <center> <img src="/assets/img/posts/soft_thresholding.png" alt="Moreau envelope" width="420px"/> <img src="/assets/img/posts/moreau_envelope.png" alt="Moreau envelope" width="420px"/> </center> <div class="caption"> Fig. 1: (left) Moreau envelope of the absolute value function. (right) Soft-thresholding operator. </div> <p>Finally, by injecting \eqref{eq:soft} into \eqref{eq:3}, the Moreau envelope of the absolute value function can be written as</p> \[ egin{align} \mathrm{M}_\lambda |x| &amp;= |\mathrm{sign}(x) \max\{ |x|-\lambda,0 \}| + 	inv{2\lambda} (x-\mathrm{sign}(x) \max\{ |x|-\lambda,0 \})^2 \ &amp;=  egin{cases} &amp;	inv{2\lambda} x^2,~~|x|\le \lambda, \ &amp;x- rac{\lambda}{2},~~x&gt;\lambda \ &amp;-x- rac{\lambda}{2},~~x&lt;-\lambda \ \end{cases} \ &amp;=  egin{cases} &amp;	inv{2\lambda} x^2,~~|x|\le \lambda, \ &amp;|x|- rac{\lambda}{2},~~|x|&gt; \lambda, \ \end{cases}. \end{align}\] <hr/> <p>The <em>soft-thresholding</em> operator is of great interest because it is often involved in minimization problems of the form</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:inv_prob} 	ilde{ s x} = \mathrm{argmin}_{ s x} 	inv 2 \|  s y -  s{Ax} \|_2^2 + \lambda \|  s x \|_1, \end{equation}\] <p>where \(\lambda \|  s x \|_1\) is a <em>convex</em> but <em>nondifferentiable</em> function so that we cannot compute their gradient. Eq. \eqref{eq:inv*prob} can be solved efficiently by the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proximal_gradient_methods_for_learning"><em>proximal gradient method</em> (PGM)</a> that consists of alternating because a gradient descent step with respect to the differentiable function and a <em>proximal</em> step with respect to the nondifferentiable function(s). For instance, \eqref{eq:inv_prob} can be solved by the iterations</p> \[ egin{equation}  s x^{(k+1)} = \cl S_\lambda  ig( s x^{(k)} - \alpha  s A^* ( s{Ax}^{(k)} -  s y)  ig). \end{equation}\]

