ewcommand{	inv}[1]{ rac{1}{#1}} 
ewcommand{\intfty}{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}} 
ewcommand{\iintfty}{\iint_{-\infty}^{\infty}} 
ewcommand{\so}{\sin (	heta)} 
ewcommand{\co}{\cos (	heta)}\] <blockquote> <p><strong>Definition. (Radon transform)</strong> The Radon transform \(\cl R \{f\}\) of $f: \Rbb^2 ightarrow \Rbb$ is</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:radon} \cl R \{f\}(t, 	heta) = \iintfty f(x,y) \delta(t-x \co - y \so) \ud x \ud y, \end{equation}\] <p>for $t \in \Rbb$ and $0\le 	heta &lt; \pi$.</p> </blockquote> <center> <img src="/assets/img/posts/fourier_slice_theorem.png" alt="fourier-slice" width="500px"/> </center> <div class="caption"> Fig. 1: Illustration of the Fourier-slice theorem. </div> <p>We wish to prove the <em>Fourier-Slice Theorem</em> reminded in p.37 of <a class="citation" href="#adcock">(Adcock &amp; Hansen, 2021)</a></p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:fourier_slice} \cl{F}_1  ig\{ \cl R \{ f \}  ig\} (\omega, 	heta) = \cl F \{ f \} (\omega \co, \omega \so),~~~~\omega \in \Rbb,~ 	heta \in [0,2\pi) \end{equation}\] <p>Where $\cl F$ denotes the 2-D Fourier transform and $\cl F_1$ is the 1-D Fourier transform with respect to the first component.</p> <p><em>proof</em>.</p> <p>Using \eqref{eq:radon} and the definition of the Fourier transform, the left term of \eqref{eq:fourier_slice} writes as</p> \[ egin{align} \label{eq:left} \cl{F}_1  ig\{ \cl R \{ f \}  ig\} (\omega, 	heta) &amp;= \intfty \left( \iintfty f(x,y) \delta(t-x \co -y \so) \ud x \ud y ight) e^{-\im \omega t} \ud t \ &amp;= \iintfty f(x,y)  ig( \intfty \delta(t-x \co -y \so) e^{-\im \omega t} \ud t  ig) \ud x \ud y \ &amp;= \iintfty f(x,y)  ig( \intfty \delta(t-x \co -y \so) e^{-\im \omega (x \co+y\so)} \ud t  ig) \ud x \ud y \ &amp;= \iintfty f(x,y) e^{-\im \omega (x \co+y\so)} \underbrace{ ig(\intfty \delta(t-x \co -y \so) \ud t  ig)}_{=1} \ud x \ud y \ &amp;= \iintfty f(x,y) e^{-\im \omega (x \co+y\so)} \ud x \ud y \ &amp;= \cl F \{ f \} (\omega \co, \omega \so) \end{align}\] <p>which proves \eqref{eq:fourier_slice}. □</p> <h2 id="references">References</h2> <h2 class="bibliography">2021</h2> <ol class="bibliography"><li><div class="row"> <div class="col col-sm-2 abbr"> </div> <div id="adcock" class="col-sm-8"> <div class="title">Compressive imaging: structure, sampling, learning</div> <div class="author"> Ben Adcock and Anders C Hansen </div> <div class="periodical"> 2021 </div> <div class="periodical"> </div> <div class="links"> </div> </div> </div> </li></ol>

\[
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\ud}{\mathrm{d}} 
ewcommand{\Rbb}{\mathbb{R}} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{	inv}[1]{ rac{1}{#1}} 
ewcommand{\indep}{\perp \!\! \perp}\] <p>In the following derivations, we show that the typical regularised inverse problem to recover a quantity $ s x \in \Rbb^N$ from noisy observations of the form $ s y =  s{Hx} +  s n \in \Cbb^M$ with additive noise $ s n \in \Cbb^M$ corresponds to the <em>maximum a posteriori</em> (MAP) estimator when a prior distribution \(p( s x) = e^{-	heta \|  s{\Psi x} \|_1}\) is assumed on the data:</p> \[ egin{align} \hat x_{	ext{MAP}} &amp;= \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ p( s x| s y) = \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~  rac{p( s y| s x) p( s x)}{p( s y)} \underset{p( s y) \indep  s x}{=} \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ p( s y| s x) p( s x) \ &amp;= \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ e^{-	inv 2 \| s y- s{Hx}\|_2^2} e^{-	heta \| s{\Psi x}\|_1} = \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ e^{-	inv 2 \| s y- s{Hx}\|_2^2 -	heta \| s{\Psi x}\|_1} \ &amp;= \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ \log  ig(e^{-	inv 2 \| s y- s{Hx}\|_2^2 -	heta \| s{\Psi x}\|_1}  ig) \ &amp;= \mathrm{argmin}_{ s x}~ \underbrace{	inv 2 \| s y- s{Hx}\|_2^2}_{	ext{Data fidelity}} + \underbrace{	heta \| s{\Psi x}\|_1}_{	ext{Regularization}}. \end{align}\] <p>Additionnally, we define <em>Bayesian point estimators</em> that arise from the decision ``what point $\hat{ s x} \in \Rbb^N$ summarises $ s x| s y$ best?’’. The optimal decision under uncertainty is</p> \[ egin{equation*} \hat{ s x}_L = \mathrm{argmin}_{ s u \in \Rbb^N}~ \mathbb{E}[L( s u, s x)| s y] = \mathrm{argmin}_{ s u \in \Rbb^N}~ \int L( s u, s x) p( s x| s y) \ud  s x \end{equation*}\] <p>where the loss $L( s u, s x)$ measures the “dissimilarity” between $ s u$ and $ s x$.</p> <p>General desiderata:</p> <ul> <li>$L( s u, s x) \ge 0,~  orall  s u,  s x \in \Rbb^N$,</li> <li>$L( s u, s x)=0 \Leftrightarrow  s u= s x$,</li> <li>$L$ strictly convex w.r.t. $ s u$ (for estimator uniqueness).</li> </ul> <p><strong><u>Example:</u></strong></p> <p>The <em>minimum mean square error</em> (MMSE) estimator, taking \(L( s u, s x) = \| s u- s x\|_2^2\), is obtained as:</p> \[ egin{align*}  egin{split} \hat{ s x}_{	ext{MMSE}} &amp;= \mathrm{argmin}_{ s u}~ \int \| s u- s x\|_2^2 p( s x| s y) \ud  s x \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow \int (\hat{ s x}_{	ext{MMSE}}- s x) p( s x| s y) \ud  s x=0 \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow \hat{ s x}_{	ext{MMSE}} \underbrace{\int p( s x| s y) \ud  s x}_{=1} = \int  s x p( s x| s y) \ud  s x \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow \hat{ s x}_{	ext{MMSE}} = \mathbb{E}[ s x| s y]. \end{split} \end{align*}\]
